---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, whatsappLink } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title={`Privacy, plainly — ${site.name}`}
  description="What runs inside your house, what leaves it, and what we can and cannot see. In plain language, not legal boilerplate."
  waContext="privacy"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="brass-rule"></div>
      <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:16px">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="dsp" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5.4vw,4rem)">
        What leaves<br />your house
      </h1>
      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:22px">
        <p class="lede">
          Around a third of people who decide against a smart home say privacy is
          why. That is a reasonable worry and it deserves a real answer rather
          than a policy nobody reads.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="measure">
        <p
          class="accent-line"
          style="font-size:clamp(1.4rem,3vw,2.1rem);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);padding-top:24px"
        >
          You should be able to have the automation without the surveillance.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top:34px">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="eyebrow">Stays in the house</span>
          <h3 class="dsp">Local by default</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Switch and scene logic, wherever the hardware supports running it locally</li>
            <li>Camera recording, if you choose local storage</li>
            <li>Sensor readings driving automations in the same house</li>
            <li>Everything, during an internet outage — the house keeps working</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="eyebrow">Leaves the house</span>
          <h3 class="dsp">Only what has to</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Remote access when you open the app away from home</li>
            <li>Voice commands, if you install voice — processed by Amazon or Google, not us</li>
            <li>Cloud clips, only if you ask for them</li>
            <li>Firmware checks</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="eyebrow">What we can see</span>
          <h3 class="dsp">Us, specifically</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Nothing, unless you are on a service plan with remote monitoring</li>
            <li>On a plan: device health — online, offline, battery, firmware</li>
            <li>Never your camera feeds</li>
            <li>Never your voice recordings</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sechead"><h2 class="dsp">Choices you get to make</h2></div>
      <div class="worries" style="margin-top:26px">
        <div class="worry">
          <h3>Voice is optional, room by room</h3>
          <p>
            No microphone goes into a room you did not ask for one in. A house with
            no voice at all still does everything through the wall switches, the
            app and the schedules.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="worry">
          <h3>Cameras can be local-only</h3>
          <p>
            We will happily configure recording to a drive in your house with
            nothing going to a cloud service. You lose remote viewing; you keep
            the footage entirely.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="worry">
          <h3>The devices get their own network</h3>
          <p>
            As standard, we put smart home hardware on a separate network from
            your phones and laptops. It is the single most effective thing anyone
            can do about connected-device risk, and it costs nothing extra.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="worry">
          <h3>The accounts are yours</h3>
          <p>
            Every account is created in your name, with your email, and you hold
            the credentials. We do not keep a back door into houses we have
            finished, and we will remove our access on request at any time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="measure stack g16">
        <div class="sechead"><h2 class="dsp">This site</h2></div>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-2);margin-top:6px">
          The estimator runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you enter is sent
          anywhere until you tap the WhatsApp button, and at that point you can
          see and edit the message before it goes.
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          TODO — before launch, add the legal notice covering analytics, contact
          form storage and applicable data protection law. This page is the plain
          language version and should stay short; the formal notice can sit
          underneath it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--paper band--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="stack g16 measure">
        <div class="brass-rule"></div>
        <h2 class="dsp">Ask us something specific</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          If there is a particular thing you do not want in your house, say so
          before the survey. It is far easier to design around than to remove.
        </p>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:8px">
          <a class="btn btn--wa" href={whatsappLink('a privacy question')} rel="noopener">
            Ask on WhatsApp
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
